<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biloxi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biloxi is a city on the Gulf Coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In 1699 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-maurepas">Fort Maurepas</a> across Biloxi Bay, the first French settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, and a later settlement on the present site served as the colony's capital from 1720 to 1722. It grew into a seafood and resort town and was badly damaged by Hurricane Katrina in 2005.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biloxi.jpg" alt="Biloxi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Woodlot · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BiloxiLightHouseandVisitorsCenter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Biloxi lies on the Gulf Coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in Harrison County, on the Mississippi Sound, with barrier islands off the shore. It is bordered partly on the northeast by Biloxi Bay, across which lie Ocean Springs and St. Martin, and Gulfport adjoins it on the west. The climate is humid subtropical, and tropical storms strike in late summer and autumn. The French name Bilocci transliterates the name of the local Native American tribe in their own language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1699 French colonists under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a> formed the first permanent European settlement in French Louisiana at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-maurepas">Fort Maurepas</a>, now in Ocean Springs and called Old Biloxi. The present city's site was first settled in 1720 around Fort Louis, and the capital of French Louisiana moved there from Mobile. In 1722, fearing tides and hurricanes, Governor Bienville moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>. France ceded its colonies east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in 1763; British rule lasted until 1779 and Spanish rule until 1810, yet Biloxi remained mostly French in character. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took over the area in 1811, and Mississippi became a state in 1817.</p><p>In the 19th century Biloxi became a summer resort. The Biloxi Lighthouse was built in Baltimore and finished on the site in May 1848. Union forces took Ship Island early in the Civil War and with it control of Biloxi, which suffered no direct damage. The first seafood cannery opened in 1881 and drew immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Keesler Field, built during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, became a major training site. In 1959 the physician Gilbert R. Mason led the Biloxi Wade-Ins against the exclusion of black people from the beach; a riot in 1960 killed ten people, and the protests led to desegregation of the beaches. Legal gambling in the 1990s made the city a centre of resort casinos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Biloxi had a population of 49,449 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is one of the two principal cities of the Gulfport–Biloxi metropolitan area. It has six historic districts on the National Register of Historic Places, and its Blessing of the Fleet, begun in 1929, takes place each year before the shrimp season. Katrina's storm surge of 30 feet on 29 August 2005 caused massive damage, and the most destructive earlier hurricanes struck in 1855, 1906, 1909, 1947 and 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Biloxi, Mississippi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlement began when Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville built Fort Maurepas at <b>Biloxi</b> in 1699.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-louisiana">French Louisiana</a></li></ul></section>
